    "title": "General or Local Anaesthesia in One-Day Thyroid Surgery-Does It Matter?",
    "views": 7067,
    "pdfUrl": "js/data/pdfs/498.pdf",
    "volume": 29,
    "authors": [
      {
        "name": "Maya Belitova",
        "orcid": "",
        "affiliation": "Department of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Care, University Hospital “Queen Giovanna”-Sofia, Sofia, Bulgaria"
      },
      {
        "name": "Dimitar Karadimov",
        "orcid": "",
        "affiliation": "Department of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Care, University Hospital “Queen Giovanna”-Sofia, Sofia, Bulgaria"
      },
      {
        "name": "Rumen Pandev",
        "orcid": "",
        "affiliation": "Department of One-Day Surgery, University Hospital “Queen Giovanna”-Sofia, Sofia, Bulgaria"
      }
    ],
    "abstract": "Abstract Objective: We aimed to determine the safety and feasibility of general anaesthesia (GA) compared with local anaesthesia + Monitored Anaesthesia Care (LA+MAC) for One-Day Thyroid Surgery (ODTS); to assess patient and operator satisfaction with either of the anesthetic approaches. Material and Methods: We reviewed prospectively 130 patients who underwent ODTS from 2008 to 2011. 64 patients were operated on under GA and 62 - under LA+MAC. All variables of patient demographic characteristics, duration and type of operation, postoperative complications, difficulty in airway management and postoperative opiate consumption were recorded and analyzed. Results: There is no difference in respect to length of stay, discharge time and major/minor complications rate between two groups, but in the LA+MAC group, pain appears earlier and is more severe (56min; VAS 6, 5) than in GA patients (223 min; VAS 1, 5; p<0.001). Patient satisfaction was similar but the operator preference was greater for GA. Conclusion: In experienced hands LA with MAC for Thyroidectomy is a safe and wise choice, but GA is even more so!",
    "accepted": "",
    "featured": false,
    "keywords": [],
    "received": "",
    "citations": 0,
    "downloads": 9934,
    "published": "2012-01-01",
    "hasFullText": true,
    "imageCorner": false,
    "localPdfUrl": "js/data/pdfs/498.pdf",
    "previewText": "Abstract Objective: We aimed to determine the safety and feasibility of general anaesthesia (GA) compared with local anaesthesia + Monitored Anaesthesia Care (LA+MAC) for One-Day Thyroid Surgery (ODTS); to assess patient and operator satisfaction with either of the anesthetic approaches. Material and Methods: We reviewed prospectively 130 patients who underwent ODTS from 2008 to 2011. 64 patients were operated on under GA and 62 - under LA+MAC. All variables of patient demographic characteristics, duration and type of operation, postoperative complications, difficulty in airway management and postoperative opiate consumption were recorded and analyzed. Results: There is no difference in respect to length of stay, discharge time and major/minor complications rate between two groups, but in the LA+MAC group, pain appears earlier and is more severe (56min; VAS 6, 5) than in GA patients (223 min; VAS 1, 5; p<0.001). Patient satisfaction was similar but the operator preference was greater for GA. Conclusion: In experienced hands LA with MAC for Thyroidectomy is a safe and wise choice, but GA is even more so!",
    "abstractHtml": "<div class=\"col-lg-12 col-md-12 col-sm-12 col-xs-12\">\n\t\t\t<h3>\r\n\tAbstract</h3>\r\n<p>\r\n\t<strong>Objective</strong>: We aimed to determine the safety and feasibility of general anaesthesia (GA) compared with local anaesthesia + Monitored Anaesthesia Care (LA+MAC) for One-Day Thyroid Surgery (ODTS); to assess patient and operator satisfaction with either of the anesthetic approaches.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\t<strong>Material</strong> <strong>and Methods</strong>: We reviewed prospectively 130 patients who underwent ODTS from 2008 to 2011. 64 patients were operated on under GA and 62 - under LA+MAC. All variables of patient demographic characteristics, duration and type of operation, postoperative complications, difficulty in airway management and postoperative opiate consumption were recorded and analyzed.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\t<strong>Results</strong>: There is no difference in respect to length of stay, discharge time and major/minor complications rate between two groups, but in the LA+MAC group, pain appears earlier and is more severe (56min; VAS 6, 5) than in GA patients (223 min; VAS 1, 5; p&lt;0.001). Patient satisfaction was similar but the operator preference was greater for GA.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\t<strong>Conclusion</strong>: In experienced hands LA with MAC for Thyroidectomy is a safe and wise choice, but GA is even more so!</p>\r\n \t\t\t\t\t\t        \t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t</div>",
