    "abstract": "Abstract A 20-year-old male patient was admitted to emergency department with complaints of being unable to swallow and urinate and wounds in his hands, feet, mouth and penis. He had purulent conjunctivitis, hyperemia in nasal mucosa, pseudomembranous stomatitis in his mouth, inflammation in external urethral orifice, vesiculobullous lesions in both hands and feet. Nikolsky sign was positive. The patient was diagnosed as Stevens- Johnson syndrome and hospitalized in intensive care unit. Intravenous fluid replacement treatment, ceftriaxone sodium, ranitidine, nystatin and methylprednisolone were started. In addition, he received five sessions of hyperbaric oxygen therapy (at 2.5 atmospheres absolute for 90 minutes/day). The lesions were markedly improved after three days and he was completely healed after five days of treatment. In this case report, the potential role of hyperbaric oxygen therapy in the treatment of cutaneous and mucosal manifestations of Stevens-Johnson syndrome was discussed.",
